---
title: "Filtering rows"
description: "Predicates, null handling, and the one rule that catches everyone."
---

## Building a predicate {#sec-filter-predicate}

`Frame.filter` takes an expression that evaluates to a boolean per row. Build it from
`col()` and the usual comparison operators:

```python
recent = sales.filter(tarn.col("date") > "2024-01-01")
```

Combine predicates with `&` and `|`, and negate with `~`. Python's `and`/`or`/`not`
do **not** work here: they coerce their operands to a single `bool`, which an
expression cannot meaningfully become, so Tarn raises `AmbiguousExprError` rather
than guessing. Parenthesise the operands, because `&` binds tighter than `>`:

```python
q = sales.filter((tarn.col("date") > "2024-01-01") & (tarn.col("region") == "north"))
```

## How nulls behave {#sec-filter-nulls}

A comparison against null is null, not false, and `filter` keeps only rows where the
predicate is *true*. A null therefore drops the row, which is usually what you want
and occasionally a silent data loss you did not intend.

Consider a `discount` column where a missing value means "no discount applied". The
predicate `col("discount") < 0.5` drops every row with a null discount, even though
those rows arguably satisfy it. Written out, the three-valued logic is:

| `discount` | `discount < 0.5` | row kept? |
|------------|------------------|-----------|
| `0.2`      | true             | yes       |
| `0.8`      | false            | no        |
| `null`     | null             | no        |

To keep the nulls, say so explicitly with `is_null`:

```python
q = sales.filter((tarn.col("discount") < 0.5) | tarn.col("discount").is_null())
```

Or fill them first, which is clearer when the fill value is meaningful in its own
right rather than a trick to make a predicate behave:

```python
q = sales.with_column(tarn.col("discount").fill_null(0.0)).filter(tarn.col("discount") < 0.5)
```

The same rule applies to `!=`, which surprises people more than `==` does. A row
whose `region` is null is dropped by `col("region") != "north"`, so a filter and its
apparent complement do not partition the frame: rows can fall out of both halves.
When you are splitting a frame into two parts and expect the row counts to add up,
this is the reason they do not.

Aggregations take the opposite convention: `sum`, `mean` and friends skip nulls
rather than propagating them, so `sum` over a column of all nulls is `0` and not
null. The asymmetry is deliberate — a filter that silently kept unknown rows would
corrupt a query, whereas an aggregate that refused to produce a number whenever one
input was missing would be useless on real data. We call the resulting behaviour
**null-dropping-filter** semantics, and it is the single convention worth memorising
from this chapter.

## Filtering after a group {#sec-filter-post-group}

`filter` before an aggregation restricts the rows that go in; `having` restricts the
groups that come out. Reaching for `filter` after `group_by` is the usual mistake:

```python
q = sales.group_by("region").agg(tarn.col("revenue").sum()).having(tarn.col("revenue_sum") > 100)
```
